1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
Chapter 9 lecture.

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:12,800
Okay, we're going to look very closely at slang today, and in particular, slang in English in the United States.

3
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:26,800
So, first we'll look at the meaning of slang, and then we'll look at a number of reasons why people use slang.

4
00:00:26,800 --> 00:00:34,800
And then I want to talk a bit about how the slang is created, so how slang is made.

5
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:40,800
In fact, most slang in any language is created by young people.

6
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:42,800
So the first thing is, what is slang?

7
00:00:42,800 --> 00:00:49,800
Okay, slang is the term for new and informal words and phrases.

8
00:00:49,800 --> 00:00:54,800
And these words and phrases tend to come from different groups within society.

9
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:57,800
Let me say that again for you.

10
00:00:57,800 --> 00:01:06,800
Slang is new and informal words or phrases which tend to come from certain groups in a society.

11
00:01:06,800 --> 00:01:10,800
Let me move on to describe slang for you.

12
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:18,800
Now, in general, we use slang to say something new or to say something old in an unusual way.

13
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:26,800
So a quick example is, well, when people say, "I'm pumped up," or "I'm psyched,"

14
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:31,800
both of these expressions someone might say to mean, "I'm really excited."

15
00:01:31,800 --> 00:01:38,800
Now, slang, as you probably know, can either be playful and funny.

16
00:01:38,800 --> 00:01:41,800
You know, it's something you can use with friends.

17
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:49,800
But on the other hand, it can sometimes be impolite and offensive.

18
00:01:49,800 --> 00:01:52,800
So it can have many different effects.

19
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:57,800
All right, so let's move on to why people want to use slang.

20
00:01:57,800 --> 00:01:59,800
Well, there are a number of reasons.

21
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:08,800
One important reason people use slang is that slang makes people feel connected to

22
00:02:08,800 --> 00:02:12,800
or part of a peer group.

23
00:02:12,800 --> 00:02:16,800
So for instance, let's say there's a group of friends who spend a lot of time together.

24
00:02:16,800 --> 00:02:18,800
Maybe they go to the same school.

25
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:23,800
They use more slang with each other because it makes them feel connected.

26
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:25,800
It's like they have their own language.

27
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:33,800
So in essence, when they use their slang together, they're showing their group identity,

28
00:02:33,800 --> 00:02:35,800
like wearing the same jackets.

29
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:39,800
They're showing that they belong to the same group.

30
00:02:39,800 --> 00:02:49,800
Also, and this is a key point, people use slang to show that they're friendly

31
00:02:49,800 --> 00:02:51,800
and can be casual with each other.

32
00:02:51,800 --> 00:02:54,800
This is especially common in the workplace.

33
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:58,800
Now, some people use slang to make their speech more colorful

34
00:02:58,800 --> 00:03:02,800
or to make a certain kind of impression on other people.

35
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:07,800
So these are a few reasons why slang is used.

36
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:12,800
Now, let's move on to discuss how slang is created.

37
00:03:12,800 --> 00:03:19,800
What I want to look at is how people create new slang expressions

38
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:22,800
and often change the spoken language.

39
00:03:22,800 --> 00:03:27,800
Okay, now, slang is created in a variety of different and interesting ways.

40
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:32,800
One way slang is created is by taking an old word and giving it a new meaning,

41
00:03:32,800 --> 00:03:37,800
like the slang word "wired."

42
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:48,800
Wired.

43
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:51,800
So let's look at how this works.

44
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:54,800
You know the word "wire," like the wire in an electrical cord.

45
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:58,800
To be "wired" is an adjective.

46
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:02,800
It means the way you feel when you've had too much coffee.

47
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:06,800
You feel too excited and maybe even a little crazy.

48
00:04:06,800 --> 00:04:08,800
You feel wired.

49
00:04:08,800 --> 00:04:10,800
You feel full of energy.

50
00:04:10,800 --> 00:04:16,800
So that's an example of a new slang word created from an old word.

51
00:04:16,800 --> 00:04:22,800
Okay, there's another way that slang is made, and that's by creating a new word,

52
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:25,800
a word that didn't exist before.

53
00:04:25,800 --> 00:04:30,800
And the interesting thing here is that these words usually come from subcultures

54
00:04:30,800 --> 00:04:34,800
or smaller groups from inside a culture.

55
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:41,800
A really good example of creating a new word is the slang word "bling bling."

56
00:04:41,800 --> 00:04:53,800
[Slang word "bling bling" is used to describe a person who is in a situation of anxiety and anxiety.]

57
00:04:53,800 --> 00:04:57,800
This is a slang word that means "shiny jewelry."

58
00:04:57,800 --> 00:04:59,800
It's a noun.

59
00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:02,800
Most people first heard "bling bling" in a hip-hop song some years ago,

60
00:05:02,800 --> 00:05:09,800
and suddenly many young people began using this expression in their regular conversations.

61
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:15,800
So for example, someone might say, "Look at that bling bling she's wearing."

62
00:05:15,800 --> 00:05:24,800
So we've talked about creating slang by using old words in new ways, by creating new words.

63
00:05:24,800 --> 00:05:30,800
And now let's look at a third way that slang enters the language.

64
00:05:30,800 --> 00:05:36,800
The third way slang is made is by borrowing a word from another language,

65
00:05:36,800 --> 00:05:41,800
or by borrowing from another culture.

66
00:05:41,800 --> 00:05:43,800
This is what I'm interested in.

67
00:05:43,800 --> 00:05:50,800
Slang in the United States incorporates hundreds of words from languages of the people who have come to live here,

68
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:57,800
like German people, Italian, Chinese, just to name a few.

69
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:07,800
Okay, now it's important to note that not everyone who speaks English speaks the same slang.

70
00:06:07,800 --> 00:06:10,800
There are a few factors that create differences in slang.

71
00:06:10,800 --> 00:06:14,800
So right now, let's take a look at a couple of these factors.

72
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:22,800
The first important factor is region, meaning the part of a country where a person is from.

73
00:06:22,800 --> 00:06:30,800
So for instance, you might hear a person from the southern states say, "Hey, y'all," which means, "Hello, everybody."

74
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:33,800
But a person from California might say, "What's up, guys?"

75
00:06:33,800 --> 00:06:36,800
So different regions, different slang.

76
00:06:36,800 --> 00:06:38,800
That's the bottom line there.

77
00:06:38,800 --> 00:06:40,800
Okay, here's another factor.

78
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:42,800
It's people's occupation.

79
00:06:42,800 --> 00:06:46,800
Jobs. Jobs can have different slang, too.

80
00:06:46,800 --> 00:06:51,800
A great deal of job-specific slang is also relevant to others.

81
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:54,800
So eventually it becomes popular in regular society.

82
00:06:54,800 --> 00:06:59,800
In other words, it eventually becomes general slang.

83
00:06:59,800 --> 00:07:02,800
Are there any questions?

84
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:03,800
Excellent.

85
00:07:03,800 --> 00:07:13,800
Okay, well, so far I've only talked about slang in a positive way, but believe it or not, slang can sometimes be controversial.

86
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:15,800
That's right.

87
00:07:15,800 --> 00:07:19,800
Not everyone has a positive opinion about slang.

88
00:07:19,800 --> 00:07:28,800
For instance, some people think that if people use too much slang, eventually it might destroy the English language.

89
00:07:28,800 --> 00:07:30,800
Let me illustrate this.

90
00:07:30,800 --> 00:07:36,800
Remember I said that a lot of slang is created by and used by young people.

91
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:43,800
A lot of the slang in style today and used by young people is not grammatically correct.

92
00:07:43,800 --> 00:07:46,800
It breaks the rules of the language.

93
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:51,800
Of course, young people want to break the rules of the language.

94
00:07:51,800 --> 00:08:00,800
They want to do things differently because this shows that they are new, a new generation.

95
00:08:00,800 --> 00:08:03,800
They don't want to follow the traditions of society.

96
00:08:03,800 --> 00:08:11,800
A good example of youth slang that is ungrammatical is the popular expression "my bad."

97
00:08:11,800 --> 00:08:21,800
My bad.

98
00:08:21,800 --> 00:08:24,800
My bad.

99
00:08:24,800 --> 00:08:28,800
It's what you say when you want to show that you are responsible for a mistake.

100
00:08:28,800 --> 00:08:35,800
Like if you forget to bring your friend his book, but it's not correct grammar.

101
00:08:35,800 --> 00:08:39,800
We should say "my mistake," but we say "my bad."

102
00:08:39,800 --> 00:08:48,800
So what I'm saying here is a lot of slang goes against the rules of the language, and some people are concerned about this.

103
00:08:48,800 --> 00:08:57,800
Some people are also concerned when slang is used in a formal situation, like when someone is giving a speech.

104
00:08:57,800 --> 00:09:01,800
They think that slang is too informal for a speech.

105
00:09:01,800 --> 00:09:04,800
They're also concerned when slang is used for writing.

106
00:09:04,800 --> 00:09:10,800
Because, as you may know, slang is used more in speaking than in writing.

107
00:09:10,800 --> 00:09:20,800
And so, as you've probably been told by your teachers, generally slang is unacceptable in academic essays.

108
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:24,800
So that's a big part of the controversy about slang.

109
00:09:24,800 --> 00:09:29,800
So to finish up, I want to make one final point.

110
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:35,800
When you're thinking about slang, keep in mind that languages are living things.

111
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:38,800
They're changing all the time.

112
00:09:38,800 --> 00:09:43,800
A new slang helps the language grow and change.

113
00:09:43,800 --> 00:09:48,800
All right. I'm afraid we're out of time. I'll see you next week.

114
00:09:48,800 --> 00:09:53,800
[ Silence ]

